the hit netflix show drive to survive  has brought formula one racing to a huge  new audience particularly in the us  weaving together the sports fierce  rivalries and electrifying action with  the aesthetics and heightened drama of  reality television it's tempting to see  a model that other sports could emulate  but in reality is that going to be  possible for streaming services  documentaries are a relatively cheap way  of getting into sports often costing  just several hundred thousand dollars  per hour of content compare that to  premier league soccer where the domestic  broadcast rights can cost in excess of  10 million dollars per game and of  course live sports have a shelf life not  many people are going to watch the  packers dolphins regular season game  three years after it was played but  people are still discovering the first  season of drive to survive several years  after it was released to understand why  drive to survive is so important to  formula one we need to look back to  2006. that's when the private equity  firm cvc acquired control of the sport  and in doing so it built up a huge  amount of debt in order to repay those  billions of dollars it had to make f1  more profitable and one of the main ways  it did so was by taking the sport behind  a paywall in markets where it was free  to air  the downside was of course that it  became hard to watch  in british households f1 was a staple of  sunday afternoon viewing after a slap up  sunday roast you might retire to the  sofa turn on the bbc and watch that  weekend's race  but in 2013 the paid tv station sky  sports acquired the rights of some of  the races and now holds all of them in  france a similar thing happened it moved  from the terrestrial channel tf1 to the  pay tv station canal plus  and audience numbers declined viewership  fell from 600 million people in 2008 to  closer to 350 million a decade later  none of this was helped by an era where  the red bull and then mercedes teams  dominated it became frankly a little bit  boring  and while the financial pop was getting  bigger there was a risk of diminishing  returns as the audience got smaller that  also made the sport less appealing to  advertisers  that's why drive to survive was such a  boon it reinvigorated flagging interest  in the sport particularly in the u.s a  market it had always struggled to crack  now there are going to be two further  races in the states in vegas and miami  and it's also provided a glimmer of hope  to other sports from tennis to rugby and  even volleyball private equity capital  is flowing to sport in a way we've never  seen before  the business plan is also largely  similar eek more money out of the  broadcast rights by either selling them  to a pay tv station or creating a new  subscription service  but again that comes with the risk of  losing fans  if you can replicate the drive to  survive model you can keep that audience  engagement  there is however a problem formula one  has some distinct advantages when it  comes to a multi-season reality tv style  approach it has a limited cast of  characters just 10 teams with two  drivers a piece and sometimes the  manager features two so at most a cast  of 30 characters  take it to soccer where each league has  400 or so players and it gets a little  bit trickier that relatively small list  of protagonists makes it easier to carve  out the rivalries that make drive to  survive so compelling  and the audience can build a  relationship with the drivers over  several seasons  amazon's all or nothing follows teams in  rugby soccer ice hockey and american  football but it moves on to a new team  each season you don't see both sides of  a given rivalry and you meet a new cast  of characters every year in drama terms  it's an anthology and its audience  numbers suffer as a consequence  other sports are still going to try in  fact the drive to survive team has just  signed a deal with netflix to produce a  show about the pga tour golf which  clearly has some similarities to f1 in  terms of the limited cast of characters  but it's also not quite the high octane  thrills and excitement that we are used  to with formula one  [Music]  you
 

